.TH CCOL_SCOPED_PTR_MP 3 "2026-07-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_scoped_ptr_mp \- declare a raw pointer that a custom allocator frees automatically at scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/common.h>

ccol_scoped_ptr_mp(name, type, mmgmt_procs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares "type *name", sets it to NULL, and registers an automatic
cleanup with __attribute__((cleanup(...))). Assign to name in the usual
way, with memory that mmgmt_procs->free accepts: usually
mmgmt_procs->malloc(size), or malloc(size) when mmgmt_procs is NULL.

At the end of the enclosing scope, the macro frees the value that name
holds with mmgmt_procs->free, or with the default allocator if
mmgmt_procs is NULL.

This macro is the custom-allocator form of ccol_scoped_ptr(). That page
describes the default-allocator form and gives the warnings that apply
to both macros: a new value inside the scope, and a release of the
ownership with ccol_scoped_ptr_release().
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name          The name of the pointer variable to declare
type          The type that the pointer points to (for example char,
              struct foo)
mmgmt_procs   The custom allocator (ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *) that frees
              the last value of name, or NULL for the default allocator
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Needs GCC or Clang, because it uses __attribute__((cleanup))

The macro stores mmgmt_procs by reference instead of copying it, so
mmgmt_procs must stay valid for at least as long as the enclosing scope
of name. That is naturally true when mmgmt_procs is a variable that is
already in scope at the point of the declaration, which is the common
case.

The cleanup tests mmgmt_procs for NULL to select mmgmt_procs->free or
the default allocator. If you give the address of a local
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t directly, the compiler can prove that the test is
always true, and GCC can then report -Waddress ("the address of X will
always evaluate as true"), which -Werror turns into an error. To
prevent this, put the address in a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t * variable and
give that variable to the macro and to the allocation.

The macro expands to two declarations, not to one expression, so use it
as its own statement. Every *_construct_scoped macro in this library
already has the same constraint.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
The pointer has no synchronisation: it is a plain local variable, and
it obeys the ordinary C rules for scope and for thread-local storage.
As for every other API with an _mp suffix in this library, the caller
is responsible for the thread safety of the functions of mmgmt_procs.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <stdlib.h>

void process(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp) {
  ccol_scoped_ptr_mp(buf, char, mp);
  buf = mp ? mp->malloc(128) : malloc(128);
  if (!buf) return;

  /* buf is used here */

}  /* mp frees buf on every return path */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr (3),
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr_release (3),
.BR ccollections (7)
